## Why This Is Asked

This question tests expertise with closures, per-id memoization, and real-time rendering patterns in frontend systems. It specifically targets race-condition handling and efficient UI updates under high-frequency data scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Closure-scoped per-id caches for memory efficiency
- Per-id memoization with bounded history management
- Promise chaining to serialize renders and handle race conditions
- Debounce using requestAnimationFrame for smooth UI updates

## Code Example

```javascript
function createDataUpdater() {
  const cache = new Map(); // id -> [last 5 results]
  let renderPromise = Promise.resolve();
  let renderScheduled = false;

  const updateCache = (id, data) => {
    const history = cache.get(id) || [];
    history.push(data);
    if (history.length > 5) history.shift();
    cache.set(id, history);
  };

  const render = () => {
    renderScheduled = false;
    // Apply cache data to UI
  };

  const scheduleRender = () => {
    if (!renderScheduled) {
      renderScheduled = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(render);
    }
  };

  return {
    processBatch: (batch) => {
      renderPromise = renderPromise.then(() => {
        batch.forEach(item => updateCache(item.id, item.data));
        scheduleRender();
      });
    }
  };
}
```

## Trade-offs

**Pros:**
- Closure ensures encapsulation and prevents external cache mutation
- Bounded cache prevents memory leaks from unbounded growth
- Promise chain guarantees render ordering consistency
- requestAnimationFrame provides optimal browser rendering timing

**Cons:**
- Added complexity compared to simple direct updates
- Memory overhead from maintaining per-id histories
- Potential render lag if batches arrive faster than processing capacity
- Cache management adds computational overhead per item

## Concept Overview
Dynamic programming optimizes recursive problems by caching subproblem results. In React, useMemo prevents expensive recalculations during re-renders.

## Implementation Details
### Top-Down Memoization (Recursive)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  const memo = {};
  const fib = (n) => {
    if (n in memo) return memo[n];
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    memo[n] = fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
    return memo[n];
  };
  return fib;
}, []);
```

### Bottom-Up Tabulation (Iterative)
```javascript
const fibonacci = useMemo(() => {
  return (n) => {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    let prev = 0, curr = 1;
    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
      [prev, curr] = [curr, prev + curr];
    }
    return curr;
  };
}, []);
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Dependency array**: Empty array [] ensures function isn't recreated
- **Memory leaks**: Large memo objects can cause memory issues
- **Over-optimization**: useMemo overhead may exceed benefits for simple calculations
- **Referential equality**: useMemo preserves function reference for child components

